    {
     "field": "explanation.walkScript",
     "before": "Line 29 has the gentlemen and the ladies pronouncing. Line 42 says his character was decided, and names nobody. Line 44 gives Mrs Bennet's dislike. Each verdict belongs to somebody in the room.",
     "after": "Lines 29 to 35 give what the gentlemen and the ladies think of him. Lines 42 to 46 give the room's verdict and Mrs Bennet's. Then lines 53 to 69 let us hear his own words to Mr Bingley. So the room speaks first, and then we hear him."
    },
    {
     "field": "option A (cmsw2f4u60070tmbvdcmgx664).value",
     "before": "We are told what the writer thinks, and not only what the room says.",
     "after": "The writer tells us herself that he is kind under all his pride."
    },
    {
     "field": "option A (cmsw2f4u60070tmbvdcmgx664).tag",
     "before": "en-narrator-voice-as-fact",
     "after": "en-plausible-not-stated"
    },
    {
     "field": "option A (cmsw2f4u60070tmbvdcmgx664).execution",
     "before": "Reads the reported verdicts as the writer's own. Line 42's 'His character was decided' names nobody as deciding it, which is the point this option misses.",
     "after": "No line says Mr Darcy is kind underneath. A reader who knows how the story ends may supply it, but the writer never says it here. She gives his looks and wealth at lines 26 to 29, his conduct at lines 39 to 42 and his rude words at lines 53 to 56 and 65 to 69, and none of them is kindness."
    },
    {
     "field": "option B (cmsw2f4u60071tmbvsbowke6z).value",
